ix v9’s bugs to check if the output quality meets requirements, otherwise I’ll adjust the batch cost calculation logic. I’ve identified 12 redundant Sinkhorn solves as the main speed bottleneck, and need to retain both required outputs with only 2 solves per pair.\n\nI'm now exploring the feasibility of obtaining batchcost from the 0.1 regularization solve, as loose quality gates may allow approximate results. I've confirmed the log(a_w) term is a constant and can be ignored during centering, so adjusting the potential sign to use f = reg * log_u will not affect the final output after centering.\n\nI’ve confirmed that adding a constant term to f before centering yields identical results to the current implementation. I’ll first build a fixed v9 version for testing, then explore further optimizations. I’ve sorted out the runtime composition, found a speedup can be achieved by cutting per-pair GeomLoss time, and noticed v9’s measured median runtime is much lower than expected.\n\nI've confirmed v10 is 3x faster than the baseline with only quality issues. I've finalized its core fixes: corrected potential sign and extra P calculation factor, keep the single log-domain solve and existing label cost. I'm exploring two regularization options for batch cost, and will prioritize testing v10's output quality first.\n\nI’ve settled on the regularization option for batch cost: I’ll prioritize Option 1 to run bug-fixed v10 and check if it passes the quality gate first. If it fails, I’ll iterate, and batch cost’s validity only matters for relative ordering to keep Q similar.\n\nHigher regularization produces smoother transport costs closer to average cost, while lower regularization aligns with true OT costs. I will first write the corrected v10 for empirical testing, and have confirmed that 0.1 reg on normalized cost equals 0.1*C_max on original cost for coupling P, with dual potentials scaled by C_max.\n\nI have finalized the four core fixes for v10, and will now start writing